  "title": "Envelope encryption with cloud KMS: key rotation traps",
  "content": "The postmortem always says the same thing: we should have caught this sooner. Teams often end up shipping key management twice — the second time because the first version failed quietly. This is the version of the write-up that includes the part that broke.\n\nYou have a cloud KMS key and you're encrypting data with it. But are you using the right key hierarchy? Envelope encryption is the standard pattern, but the devil is in the details—especially when you rotate keys. A common failure mode: teams rotate their KMS key and suddenly can't decrypt old data because they didn't store the key version alongside the ciphertext. This tutorial walks through implementing envelope encryption correctly with cloud KMS and HSMs, focusing on the rotation trap that breaks more deployments than any other.\n\n## Why encrypting data directly with a KMS key is a dead end\n\nCloud KMS services like AWS KMS, Google Cloud KMS, and Azure Key Vault are designed to protect small payloads—typically up to 4 KB. They are not meant to encrypt your database rows, files, or messages directly. The reason is both technical and economic: every Encrypt/Decrypt call is a network round-trip to a highly available, audited service, and you pay per request (and per key version). Encrypting a 1 MB file by chunking it into 4 KB pieces would mean 256 KMS calls per file, which is slow and expensive.\n\nMore importantly, KMS keys are region-scoped and have availability SLAs that are good but not perfect. If your application depends on KMS for every read, a KMS outage becomes your outage. Envelope encryption solves this by using KMS only to protect a data key, which then encrypts your actual data locally. The data key can be cached, and the ciphertext can be stored anywhere—including in a database or object store—without needing KMS for decryption if you cache the plaintext data key (though you must handle that securely).\n\nThe pattern is: generate a data key locally, use KMS to wrap (encrypt) it, store the wrapped key with the ciphertext, and discard the plaintext key. To decrypt, call KMS to unwrap the data key, then decrypt locally. This is the envelope: the data key is sealed inside the KMS key.\n\nBut here's the catch that trips up teams: when you rotate the KMS key, the wrapped data keys are still encrypted under the old key version. You need to keep the key version ID with the ciphertext so you can specify it during decryption. If you don't, KMS will try to decrypt with the current key version and fail with an error like `InvalidCiphertextException` or `IncorrectKeyException`. This is the rotation trap.\n\n## Prerequisites: IAM, key policies, and a local crypto library\n\nBefore writing code, ensure your environment has the necessary permissions and libraries. You'll need:\n\n- An IAM principal with `kms:GenerateDataKey`, `kms:Decrypt`, and `kms:Encrypt` permissions on the KMS key. For envelope encryption, `GenerateDataKey` is what you'll use to create a data key; `Decrypt` is for unwrapping.\n- A KMS key (symmetric, encryption/decryption). Note the key ID and, if you plan to rotate, enable automatic rotation (AWS KMS rotates annually by default; you can also rotate manually).\n- A cryptographic library for local encryption. In Python, `cryptography` is standard. In Go, the standard library's `crypto/aes` and `crypto/cipher` suffice. For this tutorial, we'll use Python with `cryptography` and `boto3`.\n\nInstall dependencies:\n\n```bash\npip install boto3 cryptography\n```\n\nSet up AWS credentials with permissions to call KMS. For local testing, you can use an IAM user with programmatic access, but in production use IAM roles.\n\n## Step 1: Generate a data key with KMS\n\nThe first step is to ask KMS to generate a data key. The `GenerateDataKey` API returns both a plaintext data key and a ciphertext blob (the wrapped key). You must store the ciphertext blob; the plaintext key is used only in memory.\n\n```python\nimport boto3\nfrom botocore.exceptions import ClientError\n\nkms = boto3.client('kms')\nkey_id = 'arn:aws:kms:us-east-1:123456789012:key/abcd1234-...'  # your KMS key ARN\n\ndef generate_data_key(key_id):\n    response = kms.generate_data_key(\n        KeyId=key_id,\n        KeySpec='AES_256'  # or 'AES_128'\n    )\n    return response['Plaintext'], response['CiphertextBlob'], response['KeyId']\n```\n\nThe response includes `KeyId`, which is the ARN of the key version that was used. You must store this `KeyId` alongside the ciphertext blob. If you don't, you won't know which key version to use for decryption later.\n\nWhy not just use the key ARN without version? Because when you call `Decrypt`, if you omit the `KeyId`, KMS uses the key version that was used to encrypt (it's embedded in the ciphertext blob metadata). Actually, AWS KMS embeds the key ID in the ciphertext blob, so you don't strictly need to store it separately for decryption—you can call `Decrypt` with just the `CiphertextBlob` and KMS will figure out the key. However, for auditing and for cross-account scenarios, storing the key ID is good practice. More importantly, if you use a different KMS key (e.g., in a disaster recovery region), you need to know which key to use. We'll store it.\n\n## Step 2: Encrypt your data locally with the data key\n\nNow you have a plaintext data key. Use it with a symmetric encryption algorithm. AES-256-GCM is a good choice because it provides authenticated encryption. You must generate a unique nonce (initialization vector) for each encryption operation. Never reuse a nonce with the same key.\n\n```python\nimport os\nfrom cryptography.hazmat.primitives.ciphers.aead import AESGCM\n\ndef encrypt_data(plaintext, data_key):\n    aesgcm = AESGCM(data_key)\n    nonce = os.urandom(12)  # 96-bit nonce for GCM\n    ciphertext = aesgcm.encrypt(nonce, plaintext, None)\n    return nonce + ciphertext  # prepend nonce for storage\n```\n\nYou'll store the nonce with the ciphertext. The output is the nonce concatenated with the ciphertext and tag. This is your encrypted data.\n\n## Step 3: Store the wrapped key and ciphertext together\n\nYou need to persist three things: the encrypted data (nonce + ciphertext), the wrapped data key (the `CiphertextBlob` from KMS), and the KMS key ID (the `KeyId` from the response). Optionally, store the encryption algorithm and version for future-proofing.\n\nA common pattern is to store these as a JSON object or in separate columns. For example, in a database, you might have columns `ciphertext`, `wrapped_key`, `kms_key_id`. Or you can serialize them into a single blob.\n\n```python\nimport json\nimport base64\n\ndef package_encrypted_data(nonce_ciphertext, wrapped_key, kms_key_id):\n    return json.dumps({\n        'data': base64.b64encode(nonce_ciphertext).decode('utf-8'),\n        'wrapped_key': base64.b64encode(wrapped_key).decode('utf-8'),\n        'kms_key_id': kms_key_id\n    })\n```\n\nStore this package. When you need to decrypt, you'll parse it.\n\n## Step 4: Decrypt by unwrapping the data key with KMS\n\nTo decrypt, you first call KMS `Decrypt` with the wrapped key and the key ID. Then use the plaintext data key to decrypt the data locally.\n\n```python\ndef decrypt_data(package):\n    obj = json.loads(package)\n    wrapped_key = base64.b64decode(obj['wrapped_key'])\n    kms_key_id = obj['kms_key_id']\n    response = kms.decrypt(\n        CiphertextBlob=wrapped_key,\n        KeyId=kms_key_id  # optional but recommended for clarity\n    )\n    data_key = response['Plaintext']\n    nonce_ciphertext = base64.b64decode(obj['data'])\n    nonce = nonce_ciphertext[:12]\n    ciphertext = nonce_ciphertext[12:]\n    aesgcm = AESGCM(data_key)\n    plaintext = aesgcm.decrypt(nonce, ciphertext, None)\n    return plaintext\n```\n\nNote: The `KeyId` parameter in `Decrypt` is optional. If provided, KMS verifies that the ciphertext was encrypted under that key; if not, it fails. If omitted, KMS uses the key ID embedded in the ciphertext blob. Providing it is a safety check.\n\n## The rotation trap: why old data becomes unreadable\n\nNow the critical part. Suppose you enable automatic rotation on your KMS key. AWS KMS rotates the backing key annually, creating a new key version. The key ARN remains the same, but the underlying key material changes. When you call `GenerateDataKey` after rotation, you get a data key wrapped under the new version. That's fine. But what about data encrypted before rotation? Its wrapped key is encrypted under the old version. When you call `Decrypt`, KMS will use the key version that was used to encrypt, as long as the ciphertext blob contains that information. AWS KMS ciphertext blobs do include the key ID and version, so decryption of old data still works—KMS retains old key versions for decryption.\n\nSo where's the trap? The trap is when you use a KMS key in a different region or account, or when you manually rotate by creating a new key and updating your application to use the new key ID. If you create a new KMS key (new ARN) and start using it for new data, but you don't keep the old key available for decryption, old data becomes unreadable. Similarly, if you use the `KeyId` parameter in `Decrypt` and hardcode it to the current key ARN, decryption of data encrypted under a different key will fail with `InvalidCiphertextException`.\n\nAnother common mistake: using the KMS key's alias instead of the key ID. Aliases can be updated to point to a different key, so if you store the alias and later change it, decryption may use the wrong key. Always store the key ARN or key ID, not the alias.\n\nAlso, beware of cross-region scenarios: KMS keys are regional. If you replicate data to another region, you must also replicate the wrapped data keys and ensure the KMS key is available in that region (via multi-Region keys or by re-wrapping). AWS KMS multi-Region keys allow you to use the same key material across regions, which simplifies this.\n\n## Edge cases: key policies, grants, and HSM-backed keys\n\nKey policies and IAM policies must allow the `kms:Decrypt` action for the principal decrypting. A common error is `AccessDeniedException` when the IAM policy allows `kms:Decrypt` but the key policy does not. In AWS KMS, the key policy is the primary access control; IAM policies can only allow if the key policy permits it. Always check both.\n\nIf you're using an HSM (hardware security module) backed key, such as AWS KMS custom key store backed by CloudHSM, the key material never leaves the HSM. Envelope encryption works the same way, but you may have additional latency and cost. Also, you cannot export the key material, so you're tied to that HSM cluster. Plan for backup and disaster recovery.\n\nAnother edge case: data key caching. Some SDKs (like the AWS Encryption SDK) provide data key caching to reduce KMS calls. This is safe if you understand the security trade-offs: caching plaintext data keys in memory for a short period reduces latency and cost, but increases the window of exposure if memory is compromised. Use a short TTL and limit the number of uses per key.\n\n## Verification: test rotation and decryption\n\nTo verify your implementation handles rotation, simulate a key rotation. In AWS KMS, you can enable automatic rotation and then manually rotate the key (which creates a new version). Then encrypt new data and ensure you can decrypt both old and new data.\n\nA simple test script:\n\n```python\n# Encrypt with current key version\nplaintext = b\"secret message\"\ndata_key, wrapped_key, key_id = generate_data_key(key_id)\nnonce_ciphertext = encrypt_data(plaintext, data_key)\npackage = package_encrypted_data(nonce_ciphertext, wrapped_key, key_id)\n\n# Rotate the key (via console or CLI)\n# aws kms enable-key-rotation --key-id <key-id>\n# aws kms rotate-key --key-id <key-id>  # not a real command; rotation is automatic or via new key\n\n# Decrypt old package\nassert decrypt_data(package) == plaintext\n\n# Encrypt new data with rotated key\nnew_data_key, new_wrapped_key, new_key_id = generate_data_key(key_id)\nnew_nonce_ciphertext = encrypt_data(b\"new secret\", new_data_key)\nnew_package = package_encrypted_data(new_nonce_ciphertext, new_wrapped_key, new_key_id)\nassert decrypt_data(new_package) == b\"new secret\"\n```\n\nNote: AWS KMS automatic rotation creates a new version but doesn't change the key ARN. The `rotate-key` command does not exist; rotation is either automatic (annual) or you can create a new key and update your application. The test above is illustrative; you may need to wait for rotation or trigger it manually via the console.\n\nFor a more robust test, use a local KMS emulator like LocalStack, which supports KMS operations including `GenerateDataKey` and `Decrypt`. This allows you to test rotation logic without affecting production keys.\n\n## Choosing between cloud KMS and self-managed HSM\n\nA side-by-side comparison helps decide when to use a cloud KMS versus managing your own HSM (e.g., YubiHSM, SoftHSM, or a hardware appliance).\n\n| Aspect | Cloud KMS (AWS/GCP/Azure) | Self-managed HSM |\n|--------|---------------------------|------------------|\n| Operational burden | Low (managed service) | High (patching, HA, backups) |\n| Cost model | Pay per request + key version | Capital expense + operational cost |\n| Key material control | Provider holds material (unless BYOK/HYOK) | You hold material |\n| Compliance | Meets many standards (FIPS 140-2) | Depends on module |\n| Latency | Network round-trip | Local (if on-prem) |\n| Scalability | High | Limited by hardware |\n\nUse cloud KMS when you want low operational overhead and are comfortable with the provider's security model. Use a self-managed HSM when you need full control over key material or have specific compliance requirements. Envelope encryption patterns apply to both.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n**How does envelope encryption work with AWS KMS?**\n\nEnvelope encryption with AWS KMS uses the `GenerateDataKey` API to create a data key, which is returned both in plaintext and encrypted under a KMS key. You use the plaintext data key to encrypt your data locally, then store the encrypted data key alongside the ciphertext. To decrypt, you call `Decrypt` with the encrypted data key to get the plaintext data key back, then decrypt your data locally. This keeps large payloads out of KMS and reduces cost and latency.\n\n**What happens when a KMS key is rotated?**\n\nWhen a KMS key is rotated, a new backing key version is created. The key ARN remains the same. Data encrypted under the old version can still be decrypted because KMS retains old versions for decryption. However, if you use a new KMS key (new ARN) and don't keep the old key available, old data becomes unreadable. Always store the key ID with the ciphertext and ensure old keys remain enabled for decryption.\n\n**Can I use envelope encryption with an HSM?**\n\nYes. Envelope encryption is a pattern, not a specific product. With an HSM, you generate a data key using the HSM (e.g., via PKCS#11), use it to encrypt data locally, and store the wrapped key. The HSM protects the master key. Cloud KMS services are essentially managed HSMs, so the pattern is identical.\n\n**Why do I get InvalidCiphertextException when decrypting?**\n\n`InvalidCiphertextException` typically means the ciphertext blob is corrupted, or you're using the wrong KMS key. If you specify a `KeyId` in the `Decrypt` call, ensure it matches the key that was used to encrypt. Also check that the ciphertext blob hasn't been truncated or altered. If you rotated keys manually and are using a new key ARN, old ciphertext encrypted under the old key will fail unless you use the old key ID.\n\n## Next step: audit your key IDs\n\nIn the next 30 minutes, grep your codebase for `kms.decrypt(` or equivalent calls and check whether you're passing the `KeyId` parameter. If you are, ensure it's not hardcoded to a single key ARN—instead, retrieve it from the stored metadata alongside the ciphertext. If you're not passing it, verify that your ciphertext blobs are self-describing (they are for AWS KMS) and that you have a plan for key rotation. Then, write a small test that encrypts with one key, rotates, and decrypts with the old key to confirm your application handles it.\n\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** November 2026\n",
